│                                                                    │
│                       1) What the problem is                       │
│                                                                    │
│ The issue is that rtl/components/tcdm_arbiter_2x1.sv contains a    │
│ header/license notice stating the code is confidential,            │
│ unpublished, and strictly forbidden from redistribution, yet the   │
│ file is already present in a public GitHub repository. That means  │
│ anyone cloning or mirroring the repo automatically receives        │
│ restricted material.                                               │
│                                                                    │
│                    2) Where the problem occurs                     │
│                                                                    │
│ The problem is in:                                                 │
│                                                                    │
│  • rtl/components/tcdm_arbiter_2x1.sv                              │
│                                                                    │
│ Specifically, the top-of-file comment block:                       │
│                                                                    │
│  • lines 5–9 in the provided snippet                               │
│                                                                    │
│ This is the restricted notice that conflicts with public           │
│ distribution.                                                      │
│                                                                    │
│               3) Best practices to take into account               │
│                                                                    │
│  • Do not keep confidential or non-redistributable code in a       │
│    public repository.                                              │
│  • If the file is intended to be public, replace the restrictive   │
│    notice with the appropriate open-source license/header.         │
│  • If the file is not meant to be public, remove it from the       │
│    public repo and replace it with a clean-room/public equivalent  │
│    or a stub.                                                      │
│  • Minimize changes if the issue is specifically about             │
│    legal/distribution status.                                      │
│  • Preserve functionality only if the file is still meant to       │
│    remain in the repo under a valid public license.                │
│                                                                    │
│                     4) How to fix the problem                      │
│                                                                    │
│ The correct fix is to remove the confidential/restricted copyright │
│ header and replace it with a permissive/public project license     │
│ header consistent with the repository’s licensing. Since the       │
│ provided context indicates the file “will eventually be released   │
│ under the SolderPad open hardware license,” the file should no     │
│ longer claim confidential status in the public repository.         │
│                                                                    │
